discussion_comments.comment_body:
Confirmed and fixed in 407cc99.

You are right about the control flow. The resume loop decides a task is done from its receipt, ahead of any worker:

```ts
if (
  receipt?.status === "completed" &&
  receipt.outputDir === join(output, "artifacts", task.id, `attempt-${receipt.attempt}`) &&
  (await hasArtifacts(receipt.outputDir))
) {
  completed += 1;
} else {
  pending.push(task);
}
```

A `completed` task is never pushed to `pending`, so the `rm(checkout, …)` at the top of an attempt — the only other removal in the file — is never reached, and when every task is in that state `runCampaign` returns early before the worker pool is even created. The "next attempt removes it" line in the original description only held for tasks that get re-queued, which a completed one never is. The clone survived every later campaign.

The fix retries the removal on the skip path, before the task is counted, and leaves the receipt exactly as the scan earned it:

```ts
await rm(join(output, "checkouts", task.id), {
  recursive: true,
  force: true,
}).catch(() => undefined);
completed += 1;
```

`tests-ts/multiscan.test.ts` gains `removes a checkout left behind by a completed scan's failed cleanup`, which runs the campaign twice against one injected `EACCES` and asserts all three things: the resume skips the task (`skipped: 1`, scan callback invoked exactly once), the receipt still reads `status: "completed", attempt: 1`, and `checkouts/` is empty afterwards.

Measured:

- both parts of the change reverted — `multiscan.test.ts` 14 pass / 3 fail
- only this resume-path retry reverted — 16 pass / 1 fail, failing on `expect(await readdir(checkouts)).toEqual([])` with `+ [ "leftover" ]`; full suite 740 pass / 5 skip / 1 fail
- as merged — `multiscan.test.ts` 17 pass / 0 fail, full suite 741 pass / 5 skip / 0 fail across 746 tests in 34 files
- `pnpm run types` exits 0, `pnpm run format` clean

One thing deliberately not done: `fs.rm`'s `maxRetries` on the worker's post-scan removal. It would narrow the transient `EBUSY` window, but it only retries a fixed set of codes, it cannot help when the handle outlives the process, and it is untestable through the `node:fs/promises` mock these tests use because the retry lives inside the implementation the mock replaces. The resume-path retry covers those cases including a crashed run. Worth a separate look as a latency improvement.

